{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HxerriARmpLDQmVri6NE1D","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017pWVNTqmKkhBx5hSBBsdTa","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 in a dream (simulated) paper review. Your focus: **Methods + Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification: A Controlled Empirical Study\"\nPDF path: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260517T034206/worst/review_input_f5f46a62.pdf\n\nThe paper compares three formulations for execution-free code verification:\n- CLS (per-test binary classification)\n- CWM (expected output generation, Code World Model style)\n- TRAJ (trajectory-level binary scoring)\n\nAcross two model families (Qwen3-8B, DeepSeek-R1-Qwen3-8B), two complexity levels (function-level, repo-level), with LoRA rank-16 fine-tuning on a new 87K per-test label dataset (SWE-bench PerTest).\n\nKey results claimed:\n- Function-level: all 3 formulations equivalent (~86-88% accuracy)\n- Repo-level Qwen3: CLS 82.35%, CWM collapses (53.7% unparseable, 42.9% acc on parseable)\n- Repo-level DeepSeek: CLS 74.8% (barely above 73% majority baseline), CWM 70.2% (below majority), TRAJ 78.1% mean (only formulation reliably above majority)\n- Error complementarity: oracle ensemble 90.63% at function-level\n- Efficiency: CLS is 6-9x faster than TRAJ\n- Re-repair downstream: null result across all conditions (p >> 0.05)\n\n## Review Context (Experiment Design)\nRead the experiment design file for full context:\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260517T034206/worst/review_context.yaml\n\nThis file lists all imagined experiments with expected results. Compare what the paper covers vs. what was planned.\n\n## Your Review Focus (Reviewer 1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Method description**: Are the three formulations (CLS/CWM/TRAJ) clearly defined? Are training details sufficient for reproduction? Key hyperparameters, data splits, checkpoint selection criteria?\n2. **Experimental design**: Does the study cover the necessary baselines, ablations, and dimensions? Are there obvious missing comparisons? Is the statistical methodology sound?\n3. **Data presentation**: Are tables/figures informative? Error bars / confidence intervals / significance tests adequate? Can a reader reconstruct the conclusions from the data alone?\n4. **Claims vs. evidence alignment**: Does every claim in abstract/intro have supporting experimental evidence? Are any claims overstated relative to the data?\n5. **Experiment coverage**: Compare the paper's experiments against the review_context.yaml plan. What experiments were planned but not reported? Does this affect claim support?\n\n## DO NOT evaluate:\n- Novelty / related work (Reviewer 2's scope)\n- Formatting / layout (not relevant for dream review)\n- Reproducibility checklist, code/data availability\n- Exact numerical precision (numbers are simulated)\n- Ethics/IRB\n\n## Dream Review Boundaries\nThis is a simulated paper. Data is imagined. Focus on experimental DESIGN quality, not numerical precision. Each weakness must be labeled as **idea-level** (affects research design/claims) or **presentation-level** (affects writing/pipeline quality only).\n\n## Scoring (6-point scale)\n1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Borderline Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept.\nTarget venue: top ML venue (~25% acceptance rate).\n\nCalibration rules:\n- 3+ Major issues → max Borderline Reject (3)\n- 2 Major issues → max Weak Accept (4)\n- 1 Major issue → max Accept (5)\n- 0 Major issues → can give Strong Accept (6)\n\nMajor issue = experiment scope insufficient for claims, statistical insignificance presented as contribution, core experiment unreproducible, etc.\n\n## Output Format\nProvide a structured review with:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted, with section references)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, each labeled [Major/Minor] and [idea-level/presentation-level], with justification for Major labels)\n- Questions for authors\n- Score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nKeep the review thorough but concise (~600-800 words)."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3703,"cache_read_input_tokens":47413,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3703},"output_tokens":11,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a86c915-6362-45cf-8a2e-022ec0eb5caa","uuid":"17a83e4c-1848-4d77-8060-3204c14978ad"}
