{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HxerriARmpLDQmVri6NE1D","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01G2MBTEYocVxD6tnZHKqCTu","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 in a dream (simulated) paper review. Your focus: **Novelty + Related Work + Story Consistency**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification: A Controlled Empirical Study\"\nPDF path: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260517T034206/worst/review_input_f5f46a62.pdf\n\nThe paper compares three formulations for execution-free code verification:\n- CLS (per-test binary classification)\n- CWM (expected output generation, Code World Model style)\n- TRAJ (trajectory-level binary scoring)\n\nAcross two model families (Qwen3-8B, DeepSeek-R1-Qwen3-8B), two complexity levels (function-level, repo-level), with LoRA rank-16 fine-tuning on a new 87K per-test label dataset (SWE-bench PerTest).\n\nThe paper is 7 pages. The abstract claims: formulation equivalence at function-level, model-contingent divergence at repo-level, CWM collapse is tokenizer-specific, error complementarity (oracle 90.63%), and 6-9x efficiency of CLS.\n\n## Paper Structure (from PDF)\n- Section 1: Introduction (problem + 5 contributions)\n- Section 2: Related Work (3 subsections: Execution-Free Verification, Process vs Outcome Supervision, Verification in Code Repair)\n- Section 3: Method (Task Definition for CLS/CWM/TRAJ, SWE-bench PerTest dataset, Training Setup)\n- Section 4: Experiments (4.1 Function-Level Equivalence, 4.2 Repo-Level Qwen3, 4.3 DeepSeek Repo Case Study, 4.4 Efficiency, 4.5 Error Complementarity, 4.6 Downstream Repair)\n- Section 5: Discussion\n- Section 6: Conclusion + Limitations\n\nKey data points:\n- Function-level: Qwen 88.4/87.2/86.3%, DeepSeek 86.9/86.8/84.2% (CLS/CWM/TRAJ)\n- Repo-level: CWM collapses on both models; CLS barely above majority on DeepSeek; TRAJ only reliable formulation on DeepSeek\n- Re-repair: complete null result\n- References use (?) placeholders throughout\n\n## Your Review Focus (Reviewer 2: Novelty + Related Work + Story)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the core contribution (controlled comparison of 3 verification formulations) sufficiently novel for a top ML venue? What is the delta over existing work? Are the individual formulations (CLS, CWM, TRAJ) well-established — if so, is the comparison itself a sufficient contribution?\n2. **Related Work coverage**: Are key competitors/baselines cited? Is the positioning accurate? Are there important missing references (e.g., original CWM paper, SWE-bench papers, other verification approaches)?\n3. **Story consistency**: Does the narrative from Abstract → Intro → Method → Results → Discussion → Conclusion form a coherent line? Are there contradictions between what's promised and what's delivered?\n4. **Contribution claims vs. actual findings**: The paper lists 5 contributions — do they all hold? Are any overstated? The re-repair null result is listed as Section 4.6 — is it honestly framed?\n5. **Scope vs. venue ambition**: Is this sufficient for EMNLP Main / top ML venue given the results?\n\n## DO NOT evaluate:\n- Method implementation details / experimental design specifics (Reviewer 1's scope)\n- Formatting / layout (not relevant for dream review)\n- Reproducibility checklist, code/data availability\n- Ethics/IRB\n\n## Dream Review Boundaries\nThis is a simulated paper. Data is imagined. Focus on idea quality / story / positioning. Each weakness must be labeled as **idea-level** (affects research design/claims) or **presentation-level** (affects writing/pipeline quality only).\n\nSpecial note on references: The paper uses (?) for all citations. This is a known dream paper artifact. Note it as a presentation-level issue but don't let it dominate your review.\n\n## Scoring (6-point scale)\n1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Borderline Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept.\nTarget venue: top ML venue (~25% acceptance rate).\n\nCalibration rules:\n- 3+ Major issues → max Borderline Reject (3)\n- 2 Major issues → max Weak Accept (4)\n- 1 Major issue → max Accept (5)\n- 0 Major issues → can give Strong Accept (6)\n\nMajor issue = novelty claim unverifiable due to missing related work, scope insufficient for contribution claims, story contradictions that undermine core thesis, etc.\n\n## Output Format\nProvide a structured review with:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted, with section references)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, each labeled [Major/Minor] and [idea-level/presentation-level], with justification for Major labels)\n- Questions for authors\n- Score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nKeep the review thorough but concise (~600-800 words)."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3703,"cache_read_input_tokens":47413,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3703},"output_tokens":11,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a86c915-6362-45cf-8a2e-022ec0eb5caa","uuid":"533d55e7-f09d-47a0-a7f4-470f9e3c9f44"}
