{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a59555117c66c53e0","tool_use_id":"toolu_017pWVNTqmKkhBx5hSBBsdTa","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":40990,"tool_uses":4,"duration_ms":67593},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"62c31865-aaab-40bb-9bfd-bf424f23feb4","session_id":"3a86c915-6362-45cf-8a2e-022ec0eb5caa"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019qdN6PmWKbwafCHtbwhkwo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019KyauvrPgBPWcNhZu9Fzst","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"\n# Dream Review: Reviewer 1 (Methods + Experiments)\n\n**Paper:** \"The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification: A Controlled Empirical Study\"\n**Tier:** worst\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper compares three formulations (CLS, CWM, TRAJ) for execution-free code verification across two model families and two complexity levels, trained on a new 87K per-test label dataset. The function-level results show clean equivalence across formulations, but the repo-level results are severely underpowered: DeepSeek CLS barely exceeds the majority baseline, CWM falls below it, and the formulation comparison is confounded by underlying training failure. The re-repair downstream experiment yields a complete null result. The paper is commendably honest about its limitations, but the ratio of actionable findings to experimental scope is thin for a top venue.\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Clean controlled design at function-level (Section 4.1, Table 2).** Holding model, data, and compute constant across three formulations is the right methodology. The function-level equivalence result (p > 0.1 paired bootstrap) is well-supported and genuinely informative for practitioners.\n\n- **Appropriate statistical methodology where applied (Sections 4.1, 4.3, 4.4).** TOST equivalence testing for CLS-vs-TRAJ (p = 0.007, +/-2pp margin), bootstrap CIs for the DeepSeek multi-seed analysis, and McNemar tests for re-repair are all correctly chosen. The paper reports power calculations (0.31 for 5pp effect) and acknowledges when results are underpowered rather than overclaiming.\n\n- **Transparent reporting of negative and confounded results (Sections 4.3, 4.6, Limitations).** The paper explicitly states that DeepSeek repo-level is confounded by training failure, that CLS-CWM gap is not significant (p = 0.18), and that re-repair is a null result. This intellectual honesty is valuable.\n\n- **Useful dataset contribution (Section 3.2, Table 1).** The SWE-bench PerTest dataset (87K labels, 423 instances, 16 candidates each) is a concrete resource contribution with clear construction methodology.\n\n- **Error complementarity analysis (Section 4.5).** The 90.63% oracle ensemble at function-level, with a +2.2pp gain over the best individual formulation, is a clean result that motivates future ensemble work.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n- **[Major, idea-level] Repo-level results are too underpowered to support the paper's central claims.** The abstract and introduction frame repo-level divergence as a key finding, but the evidence is weak. For Qwen3, repo-level has only CLS (82.35%) vs. a collapsed CWM (42.9% on 46.3% parseable subset) with no TRAJ comparison and no multi-seed analysis. For DeepSeek, CLS does not significantly exceed majority baseline (p = 0.71), CWM is below majority, and the CLS-CWM gap CI includes zero ([-1.8, 10.2]). Only 2 seeds with 48 effective instances yields power = 0.31. The paper correctly identifies this but still presents repo-level divergence as a contribution (Contribution 3). A study claiming to characterize \"complexity-dependent divergence\" needs sufficient statistical power at the higher complexity level to do so.\n\n- **[Major, idea-level] Qwen3 repo-level is missing TRAJ, making the three-way comparison incomplete.** Table 3 reports only CLS and CWM for Qwen3 at repo-level. TRAJ is absent entirely for the model that otherwise has the strongest results (CLS 82.35%). Since the paper's framing is a three-way formulation comparison, having only a two-way comparison on the better-performing model at the harder complexity level is a significant gap. The DeepSeek TRAJ result (Table 6) partially compensates, but on a model where the overall training recipe fails.\n\n- **[Major, idea-level] The \"tokenizer-specificity\" claim (Contribution 3, Section 5) is supported by only a two-point comparison.** Comparing Qwen3 (53.7% unparseable) vs. DeepSeek (28.5% unparseable) and attributing the difference to tokenizer properties is underspecified. These two models differ in architecture, pretraining data, distillation procedure, and tokenizer simultaneously. Without ablations isolating the tokenizer variable (e.g., same model with different tokenizers, or more model families), claiming the effect is \"tokenizer-specific\" is overstated. \"Model-specific\" would be defensible; \"tokenizer-specific\" is not.\n\n- **[Minor, idea-level] Re-repair experiment (Section 4.6) is severely underpowered and adds little to the paper.** 60 instances x 5 samples with a base rate of 18% yields negligible power to detect effects under 5pp. The review_context.yaml confirms this was known in advance (needed n >= 200+). Including it as Section 4.6 consumes space on a null result that the power analysis already predicted. Either scale it up or remove it entirely. The narrative guidance in the context file agrees: \"should be completely deleted.\"\n\n- **[Minor, presentation-level] Training details in Section 3.3 are insufficient for reproduction.** Learning rate, optimizer, batch size, number of epochs, warmup schedule, gradient accumulation, LoRA target modules, and dropout are all missing. \"Identical hyperparameters across formulations\" is stated but the actual values are not given. Checkpoint selection says \"best checkpoint by validation loss\" but does not specify validation set size, evaluation frequency, or early stopping criteria.\n\n- **[Minor, presentation-level] The efficiency analysis (Section 4.4) lacks quantitative grounding.** The \"6-9x inference speedup\" and \"8x batch capacity\" claims are stated without measurement methodology: no wall-clock times, no hardware specs beyond \"24GB GPU,\" no batch size configurations, no throughput numbers. These could be back-of-envelope calculations or empirical measurements; the reader cannot tell.\n\n- **[Minor, presentation-level] Function-level results (Table 2) lack error bars or multi-seed analysis.** Unlike DeepSeek repo-level which reports two seeds, function-level appears to be single-seed per cell. For the equivalence claim to hold, confidence intervals or multi-seed variance would strengthen it. The TOST test is reported only for CLS-vs-TRAJ on Qwen3; the other five pairwise comparisons lack formal testing.\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. Why is TRAJ missing from the Qwen3 repo-level comparison (Table 3)? Was it not run, or were results omitted?\n2. For the \"tokenizer-specificity\" claim: what specific tokenizer properties do you hypothesize cause the unparseable rate difference? Have you examined the failure modes (e.g., token boundary issues, special token handling) to support a tokenizer-level rather than model-level explanation?\n3. The review_context.yaml indicates error complementarity analysis was planned at repo-level but found to be uninterpretable due to CWM collapse. Why is this negative result not reported in the paper? It would strengthen the narrative about CWM limitations.\n4. What is the train/validation/test split for the 87K labels? How many per-test labels are in each partition, and is the split instance-level or label-level?\n\n## Score: 3 (Borderline Reject)\n\n**Justification:** Three major issues identified: (1) repo-level results are statistically underpowered to support the claimed contributions, (2) the three-way comparison is incomplete at repo-level for the better-performing model, and (3) the tokenizer-specificity claim is insufficiently supported. Per calibration rules, 3+ major issues caps the score at Borderline Reject. The paper has genuine methodological strengths (controlled design, honest reporting, appropriate statistical tests where applied), and the function-level equivalence result plus the dataset are real contributions. However, for a top venue, the repo-level story, which is the paper's main differentiator from a simple function-level benchmark study, does not have the statistical backing to support the claims made in the abstract and contributions list.\n\n## Confidence: 4/5\n\nI am confident in the assessment of experimental design quality and statistical power issues. The one point of uncertainty is whether the missing Qwen3 TRAJ repo-level result exists but was omitted from this draft, which would partially address weakness 2.\n","project":"exec-sim-repair-verifier","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260517T034206","overall_score":"borderline reject"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":683,"cache_read_input_tokens":40242,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":683,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":52,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017pWVNTqmKkhBx5hSBBsdTa","session_id":"3a86c915-6362-45cf-8a2e-022ec0eb5caa","uuid":"fdcdd239-68a5-4f5c-9c63-0220f2f14d65"}
