{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017pWVNTqmKkhBx5hSBBsdTa","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 3 (Borderline Reject)** | **Confidence: 4/5**\n\nThree major weaknesses drive the score:\n\n1. **Repo-level results are underpowered.** DeepSeek CLS does not significantly beat majority baseline (p=0.71), CLS-CWM gap CI includes zero, and power is only 0.31 for a 5pp effect with 2 seeds and 48 effective instances. Yet repo-level divergence is framed as a core contribution.\n\n2. **Incomplete three-way comparison at repo-level.** Qwen3 repo-level (Table 3) reports only CLS and CWM, with TRAJ entirely absent for the better-performing model. This breaks the paper's central \"three formulation comparison\" premise at the harder complexity level.\n\n3. **\"Tokenizer-specificity\" claim is overfit to two data points.** Qwen3 vs. DeepSeek differ in architecture, pretraining, and distillation procedure simultaneously. Attributing the unparseable rate difference specifically to tokenizer properties requires isolation that is not provided.\n\nKey strengths: clean controlled design at function-level, appropriate statistical methodology (TOST, bootstrap, McNemar), honest reporting of negative results, and a useful dataset contribution (87K labels). The function-level equivalence finding and error complementarity analysis (oracle 90.63%) are solid.\n\nReport saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260517T034206/worst/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a59555117c66c53e0 (use SendMessage with to: 'a59555117c66c53e0' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 43626\ntool_uses: 4\nduration_ms: 80232</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a86c915-6362-45cf-8a2e-022ec0eb5caa","uuid":"d76c20a3-13dc-47ac-8aa4-eb7792e9f9dc","timestamp":"2026-05-17T04:15:26.067Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 in a dream (simulated) paper review. Your focus: **Methods + Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification: A Controlled Empirical Study\"\nPDF path: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260517T034206/worst/review_input_f5f46a62.pdf\n\nThe paper compares three formulations for execution-free code verification:\n- CLS (per-test binary classification)\n- CWM (expected output generation, Code World Model style)\n- TRAJ (trajectory-level binary scoring)\n\nAcross two model families (Qwen3-8B, DeepSeek-R1-Qwen3-8B), two complexity levels (function-level, repo-level), with LoRA rank-16 fine-tuning on a new 87K per-test label dataset (SWE-bench PerTest).\n\nKey results claimed:\n- Function-level: all 3 formulations equivalent (~86-88% accuracy)\n- Repo-level Qwen3: CLS 82.35%, CWM collapses (53.7% unparseable, 42.9% acc on parseable)\n- Repo-level DeepSeek: CLS 74.8% (barely above 73% majority baseline), CWM 70.2% (below majority), TRAJ 78.1% mean (only formulation reliably above majority)\n- Error complementarity: oracle ensemble 90.63% at function-level\n- Efficiency: CLS is 6-9x faster than TRAJ\n- Re-repair downstream: null result across all conditions (p >> 0.05)\n\n## Review Context (Experiment Design)\nRead the experiment design file for full context:\n/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260517T034206/worst/review_context.yaml\n\nThis file lists all imagined experiments with expected results. Compare what the paper covers vs. what was planned.\n\n## Your Review Focus (Reviewer 1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Method description**: Are the three formulations (CLS/CWM/TRAJ) clearly defined? Are training details sufficient for reproduction? Key hyperparameters, data splits, checkpoint selection criteria?\n2. **Experimental design**: Does the study cover the necessary baselines, ablations, and dimensions? Are there obvious missing comparisons? Is the statistical methodology sound?\n3. **Data presentation**: Are tables/figures informative? Error bars / confidence intervals / significance tests adequate? Can a reader reconstruct the conclusions from the data alone?\n4. **Claims vs. evidence alignment**: Does every claim in abstract/intro have supporting experimental evidence? Are any claims overstated relative to the data?\n5. **Experiment coverage**: Compare the paper's experiments against the review_context.yaml plan. What experiments were planned but not reported? Does this affect claim support?\n\n## DO NOT evaluate:\n- Novelty / related work (Reviewer 2's scope)\n- Formatting / layout (not relevant for dream review)\n- Reproducibility checklist, code/data availability\n- Exact numerical precision (numbers are simulated)\n- Ethics/IRB\n\n## Dream Review Boundaries\nThis is a simulated paper. Data is imagined. Focus on experimental DESIGN quality, not numerical precision. Each weakness must be labeled as **idea-level** (affects research design/claims) or **presentation-level** (affects writing/pipeline quality only).\n\n## Scoring (6-point scale)\n1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Borderline Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept.\nTarget venue: top ML venue (~25% acceptance rate).\n\nCalibration rules:\n- 3+ Major issues → max Borderline Reject (3)\n- 2 Major issues → max Weak Accept (4)\n- 1 Major issue → max Accept (5)\n- 0 Major issues → can give Strong Accept (6)\n\nMajor issue = experiment scope insufficient for claims, statistical insignificance presented as contribution, core experiment unreproducible, etc.\n\n## Output Format\nProvide a structured review with:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted, with section references)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, each labeled [Major/Minor] and [idea-level/presentation-level], with justification for Major labels)\n- Questions for authors\n- Score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nKeep the review thorough but concise (~600-800 words).","agentId":"a59555117c66c53e0","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 3 (Borderline Reject)** | **Confidence: 4/5**\n\nThree major weaknesses drive the score:\n\n1. **Repo-level results are underpowered.** DeepSeek CLS does not significantly beat majority baseline (p=0.71), CLS-CWM gap CI includes zero, and power is only 0.31 for a 5pp effect with 2 seeds and 48 effective instances. Yet repo-level divergence is framed as a core contribution.\n\n2. **Incomplete three-way comparison at repo-level.** Qwen3 repo-level (Table 3) reports only CLS and CWM, with TRAJ entirely absent for the better-performing model. This breaks the paper's central \"three formulation comparison\" premise at the harder complexity level.\n\n3. **\"Tokenizer-specificity\" claim is overfit to two data points.** Qwen3 vs. DeepSeek differ in architecture, pretraining, and distillation procedure simultaneously. Attributing the unparseable rate difference specifically to tokenizer properties requires isolation that is not provided.\n\nKey strengths: clean controlled design at function-level, appropriate statistical methodology (TOST, bootstrap, McNemar), honest reporting of negative results, and a useful dataset contribution (87K labels). The function-level equivalence finding and error complementarity analysis (oracle 90.63%) are solid.\n\nReport saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260517T034206/worst/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":80232,"totalTokens":43626,"totalToolUseCount":4,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2306,"cache_read_input_tokens":40925,"output_tokens":394,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2306},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":394,"cache_read_input_tokens":40925,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2306,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2306,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":2,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":2}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"ab2d7e01266bfc54f","tool_use_id":"toolu_01G2MBTEYocVxD6tnZHKqCTu","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":51719,"tool_uses":11,"duration_ms":93475},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"4163baeb-b796-40c2-9b90-05265ef70439","session_id":"3a86c915-6362-45cf-8a2e-022ec0eb5caa"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014YWtbw9J2fPXLkNwzS8osm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TapKSC4N857qkuuBU8T7vZ","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"\n# Reviewer 2: Novelty + Related Work + Story Consistency\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper presents a controlled empirical comparison of three formulations (CLS, CWM, TRAJ) for execution-free code verification, trained on identical data with matched model capacity. The core finding is that formulations are equivalent at function-level complexity but diverge at repository-level in a model-contingent manner. The paper is honest about its limitations (null re-repair result, underpowered DeepSeek experiments, CWM not tested at its intended scale), but the novelty is thin for a top venue: the individual formulations are established, the comparison methodology is standard, and the most interesting findings (repo-level divergence, tokenizer-specificity) are confounded by training failures that the authors themselves acknowledge.\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Controlled experimental design (Section 3).** Holding model capacity, training data, and hyperparameters constant across formulations is the right methodology. The paper correctly identifies that prior work conflates formulation differences with model/data/scale confounds.\n\n- **Honest treatment of null and negative results (Sections 4.3, 4.6).** The DeepSeek training failure is acknowledged as a confound rather than swept under the rug. The re-repair null result (Table 7) is presented with appropriate statistical context (p-values, confidence intervals) rather than being spun as a positive. The Limitations paragraph in Section 6 is unusually forthcoming.\n\n- **Statistical rigor at function-level (Sections 4.1, 4.3 in source).** TOST equivalence testing, clustered bootstrap, ICC for within-problem correlation, and multi-seed evaluation are all appropriate. The variance asymmetry observation (CWM higher seed variance) is a useful secondary finding.\n\n- **Error complementarity analysis (Section 4.5, source Section (a)).** The observation that CLS over-predicts passing while CWM over-predicts failure, yielding a 90.63% oracle ensemble, is a genuinely useful finding for practitioners considering ensemble approaches.\n\n- **Efficiency quantification (Section 4.4).** The 6-9x (PDF) / 217x (source) inference speedup is a concrete, actionable result.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n- **[Major, idea-level] Insufficient novelty for top venue.** The core contribution is \"we compared three known formulations under controlled conditions.\" CLS, CWM, and TRAJ are all established approaches with existing implementations. The comparison methodology (same data, same model, LoRA fine-tuning) is standard practice. The function-level null result, while informative, is not surprising: when outputs are short and structured, generation and classification should perform similarly. The repo-level findings, which could be the paper's strongest contribution, are undermined by confounds (next point). A top-venue empirical study needs either a surprising finding that changes how practitioners think, or a scale/breadth of comparison that is definitive. This paper has neither: two model families, one of which fails to train properly, and a repo-level comparison where the most interesting formulation (CWM) is tested far below its intended operating point (LoRA on 8B vs. the original 32B mid-trained model, as the Limitations acknowledge).\n\n- **[Major, idea-level] Repo-level results are heavily confounded, undermining the central \"complexity-dependent divergence\" claim.** The paper's most interesting claim is that formulations diverge at repo-level. But on Qwen3, CWM collapse is attributed to tokenizer/format issues rather than formulation quality. On DeepSeek, *both* CLS and CWM fail to meaningfully exceed majority baseline (CLS: +0.6pp, CWM: -3.6pp, Table 6), and the paper acknowledges this is a training failure, not a formulation comparison. TRAJ is the only formulation reliably above majority on DeepSeek, but this comparison is across different seed counts (6 seeds for TRAJ vs. 2 for CLS/CWM). The paper cannot determine whether repo-level divergence reflects formulation properties or training/tokenizer artifacts, which is precisely the question it set out to answer.\n\n- **[Major, idea-level] The PDF version and LaTeX source tell substantially different stories.** The PDF describes experiments on \"Qwen3-8B\" and \"DeepSeek-R1-Qwen3-8B\" with a re-repair experiment (Section 4.6, Table 7). The LaTeX source describes experiments on Qwen3-4B/8B and DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B, with DeepSeek repo-level results listed as \"in progress\" (Table in source has \"---\" entries). The source Abstract hedges heavily (\"Whether this reflects a fundamental generation bottleneck or model-specific behavior remains open pending cross-model validation\") while the PDF Abstract states definitive claims. The Introduction source lists 4 contributions; the PDF lists 5. This inconsistency suggests the paper was assembled from different experimental stages, and the narrative was not unified after the experiments were completed. For a review, I evaluate what is in the PDF, but the source reveals that some PDF claims may not be fully supported.\n\n- **[Minor, idea-level] Re-repair null result (Section 4.6) weakens the practical motivation.** The Introduction motivates per-test CLS partly through diagnostic granularity enabling targeted re-repair. Section 4.6 shows this does not work: CLS diagnostics are indistinguishable from random diagnostics (p=0.83). This undermines one of the paper's selling points. The paper frames this honestly, but the disconnect between motivation (diagnostics are useful) and evidence (diagnostics provide no measurable benefit) weakens the story.\n\n- **[Minor, presentation-level] All citations appear as (?) in the PDF.** Every reference is broken, appearing as (?) throughout. While this is noted as a known dream paper artifact, for completeness: a submission with universally broken citations would be desk-rejected.\n\n- **[Minor, presentation-level] Related Work is adequate but could be strengthened.** The coverage of CWM, LEVER, CodeT, process supervision, and verification-in-the-loop is appropriate. However, the Related Work does not discuss reward model training approaches beyond SWE-RM (e.g., outcome reward models for code, RLHF-style verification), and the process-vs-outcome supervision analogy, while interesting, is somewhat stretched: per-test classification is not truly \"process supervision\" in the Lightman et al. sense (verifying intermediate reasoning steps), but rather a finer-grained outcome decomposition.\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. The Limitations acknowledge that the original CWM uses 32B models with mid-training on execution traces. Given that your LoRA comparison \"does not reflect CWM's full potential,\" how confident are you that the repo-level CWM collapse is a genuine formulation limitation rather than an artifact of undertrained generation capability? What would it take to rule this out?\n\n2. The PDF and source describe different model configurations (DeepSeek-R1-Qwen3-8B vs. DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B) and different experimental completeness. Which represents the actual state of the experiments?\n\n3. For the error complementarity finding: is the 90.63% oracle accuracy measured on the same seed, or aggregated across seeds? How stable is this number across seeds?\n\n4. The efficiency comparison (6-9x in PDF, 217x in source) differs substantially between versions. Which is the actual measured number, and does it account for the fact that CLS requires k forward passes (one per test) while TRAJ requires one?\n\n## Score: 3 (Borderline Reject)\n\n**Justification:** Three major issues: (1) insufficient novelty for a top venue given that the comparison uses standard methodology on known formulations, (2) the central repo-level claim is confounded by training failures the authors acknowledge, and (3) inconsistency between the PDF and source versions raises questions about which results are final. The paper's strengths are real (controlled design, statistical rigor, honest reporting), but the findings do not clear the bar for a ~25% acceptance rate venue. The function-level null result is expected; the repo-level result is inconclusive; the re-repair result is null. The paper would benefit from either (a) completing the cross-model validation with a model where CWM actually trains successfully, or (b) reframing as a negative-results / lessons-learned paper for a workshop or Findings track.\n\nPer calibration rules: 3+ major issues caps the score at Borderline Reject (3).\n\n## Confidence: 4/5\n\nI have strong familiarity with code verification, reward modeling, and empirical methodology for LLM evaluation. The one point of uncertainty is whether the PDF or source represents the true experimental state; my review evaluates primarily the PDF as submitted but flags the discrepancies.\n","project":"exec-sim-repair-verifier","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260517T034206","overall_score":"Borderline Reject (3/6)"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":686,"cache_read_input_tokens":50796,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":686,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01G2MBTEYocVxD6tnZHKqCTu","session_id":"3a86c915-6362-45cf-8a2e-022ec0eb5caa","uuid":"b2395a3b-9d26-4cf3-b207-eb7f643d776a"}
