{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014cZFrfJUdqTeHByHuorRBC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019NmsGNDSWXomQWCTidbJpg","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"a59555117c66c53e0","message":"[Round 2 — Cross-Share]\n\nReviewer 2 (Novelty + Related Work) has completed their Round 1 review. Here is R2's report:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Borderline Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Major Issues (R2):**\n\n1. **[Major, idea-level] Insufficient novelty for top venue**: Comparing three known formulations (CLS/CWM/TRAJ) with standard LoRA fine-tuning does not clear the novelty bar. The function-level null result is unsurprising (short inputs make all formulations equivalent); the repo-level divergence could be the strongest contribution but is undermined by confounds.\n\n2. **[Major, idea-level] Repo-level results heavily confounded**: The paper cannot disentangle formulation properties from model/tokenizer/training artifacts. CWM collapse on Qwen3 is attributed to tokenizer, but on DeepSeek both CLS and CWM fail to beat majority baseline due to training failure. This is the exact question the paper set out to answer but fails to answer cleanly.\n\n3. **[Major, presentation-level] LaTeX source vs PDF inconsistencies**: R2 found discrepancies between the compiled PDF and LaTeX source — different model names (DeepSeek-R1-Qwen3-8B vs DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B), different experimental completeness, different contribution counts, different efficiency numbers.\n\n**Strengths (R2):**\n- Controlled experimental design is commendable\n- Statistical rigor (TOST, clustered bootstrap, ICC)\n- Honest treatment of null results\n- Error complementarity finding (90.63% oracle ensemble)\n\n**Minor Issues (R2):**\n- All citations are (?) placeholders\n- Related Work is thin (~6 references, 3 short paragraphs)\n- Re-repair section promises downstream utility but delivers null results\n- Story arc breaks: abstract promises \"actionable guidance\" but core repo-level results are inconclusive\n\n---\n\nPlease (Reviewer 1, methods + experiments) supplement from your perspective:\n- R2 says the repo-level confounds mean the paper fails to answer its central question. From experimental design perspective, do you agree? Could the experimental design be salvaged with modifications?\n- R2 flags LaTeX/PDF inconsistencies as a major issue. Does this affect your confidence in the reported experimental results?\n- R2 questions whether the novelty is sufficient. From a methods perspective, is the controlled comparison methodology itself a contribution, even if results are inconclusive?\n\nSubmit your Round 2 supplementary comments. Keep it concise (~200-300 words)."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4277,"cache_read_input_tokens":51116,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4277},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a86c915-6362-45cf-8a2e-022ec0eb5caa","uuid":"e48a1a40-efde-4a7a-a1f3-f11e3013a9ea"}
