{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014cZFrfJUdqTeHByHuorRBC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01A3MZ7YJxkFWkSvBEsat1jj","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"ab2d7e01266bfc54f","message":"[Round 2 — Cross-Share]\n\nReviewer 1 (Methods + Experiments) has completed their Round 1 review. Here is R1's report:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Borderline Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Major Issues (R1):**\n\n1. **[Major, idea-level] Repo-level results underpowered**: DeepSeek CLS does not significantly beat majority baseline (p=0.71). CLS-CWM gap CI [-1.8, 10.2] includes zero. Power is only 0.31 for a 5pp effect with 2 seeds × 48 effective instances. Yet repo-level divergence is framed as a core contribution.\n\n2. **[Major, idea-level] Incomplete three-way comparison at repo-level**: Qwen3 repo-level (Table 3) reports only CLS and CWM — TRAJ is entirely absent for the better-performing model. This breaks the paper's central \"three formulation comparison\" premise at the harder complexity level where formulations actually diverge.\n\n3. **[Major, idea-level] \"Tokenizer-specificity\" claim overfit to two data points**: Qwen3 vs DeepSeek differ in architecture, pretraining, distillation, and tokenizer simultaneously. Attributing the CWM unparseable rate difference specifically to tokenizer properties requires controlled isolation that is not provided.\n\n**Strengths (R1):**\n- Clean controlled design at function-level\n- Appropriate statistical methodology (TOST, bootstrap, McNemar)\n- Honest reporting of negative results (re-repair, DeepSeek training failure)\n- Useful dataset contribution (87K labels from Docker re-evaluation)\n\n**Minor Issues (R1):**\n- Missing Qwen3 repo-level multi-seed results\n- Training details sparse (learning rate, warm-up, optimizer not specified)\n- No error analysis beyond aggregate metrics\n\n---\n\nPlease (Reviewer 2, novelty + related work) supplement from your perspective:\n- R1 flags the missing TRAJ comparison on Qwen3 at repo-level. From a story/novelty standpoint, how much does this incomplete comparison weaken the paper's positioning as a \"systematic comparison\"?\n- R1 notes the tokenizer-specificity claim is overfit to N=2 models. Does this affect the novelty assessment — is this finding still a useful observation even if causally unsubstantiated?\n- R1 highlights strong statistical methodology. Does the methodological rigor partially compensate for the weak novelty?\n\nSubmit your Round 2 supplementary comments. Keep it concise (~200-300 words)."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4277,"cache_read_input_tokens":51116,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4277},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a86c915-6362-45cf-8a2e-022ec0eb5caa","uuid":"ae05774c-6bb5-4257-8fc6-fb7eef9ef484"}
