{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BqZYbN57vEqXz99eDtyRjH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\n3\t% ACL style (review mode for anonymous ARR submission)\n4\t\\usepackage[review]{acl}\n5\t\n6\t% Math\n7\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n8\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n9\t\n10\t% Tables\n11\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n12\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n13\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n14\t\n15\t% Graphics\n16\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n17\t\\graphicspath{{../../artifacts/}}\n18\t\n19\t% Lists\n20\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n21\t\n22\t% Custom commands\n23\t\\newcommand{\\cls}{\\textsc{Cls}}\n24\t\\newcommand{\\cwm}{\\textsc{Cwm}}\n25\t\\newcommand{\\traj}{\\textsc{Traj}}\n26\t\\newcommand{\\fone}{F\\textsubscript{1}}\n27\t\\newcommand{\\pp}{\\,\\text{pp}}\n28\t\n29\t\\title{Classification Succeeds Where Generation Stalls:\\\\Reformulating Execution Simulation for Code Repair Verification}\n30\t\\author{Anonymous}\n31\t\n32\t\\begin{document}\n33\t\\maketitle\n34\t\n35\t\\input{abstract}\n36\t\n37\t\\input{introduction}\n38\t\n39\t\\begin{figure*}[t]\n40\t\\centering\n41\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/paper/fig1_reformulation.pdf}\n42\t\\caption{Three verification formulations. \\textbf{Left:} \\cls{} predicts binary pass/fail per test. \\textbf{Center:} \\cwm{} generates execution output, derives verdict by exact match. \\textbf{Right:} Trajectory-level scoring predicts patch-level resolved/unresolved. \\cls{} eliminates generation failure modes while preserving per-test diagnostic granularity.}\n43\t\\label{fig:reformulation}\n44\t\\end{figure*}\n45\t\n46\t\\input{method}\n47\t\n48\t\\input{experiments}\n49\t\n50\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n51\t\\centering\n52\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig2_scaling_gap.pdf}\n53\t\\caption{Gap amplification from function-level to repo-level complexity. Function-level: \\cls{}--\\cwm{} gap is 1.24\\pp{} at 4B (5 seeds), widening to 2.43\\pp{} at 8B (3 seeds, bootstrap $p{<}.01$). Repo-level same-size 4B: the gap explodes to 25.59\\pp{}, with \\cwm{} falling below the majority baseline (72.96\\%).}\n54\t\\label{fig:scaling_gap}\n55\t\\end{figure}\n56\t\n57\t\n58\t\\input{related_work}\n59\t\n60\t\\input{conclusion}\n61\t\n62\t\\bibliography{references}\n63\t\n64\t\\input{appendix}\n65\t\n66\t\\end{document}\n67\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HBi9wSE9U4rowAkjMe1Lag","session_id":"3a86c915-6362-45cf-8a2e-022ec0eb5caa","uuid":"a16a0e21-0f92-4df8-b716-dfb505c6f3d3","timestamp":"2026-05-17T04:17:03.956Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01J7RTvHRMeuZkk712oxUE21","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01V8JRjZdDPv5YJeuA21mwsH","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_16/projects/exec-sim-repair-verifier/docs/paper/method.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7839,"cache_read_input_tokens":8843,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7839,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HBi9wSE9U4rowAkjMe1Lag","session_id":"3a86c915-6362-45cf-8a2e-022ec0eb5caa","uuid":"fee1181d-5cea-48d9-85e2-ebf313a22c9c"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V8JRjZdDPv5YJeuA21mwsH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Method}\n2\t\\label{sec:method}\n3\t\n4\tLet $p$ denote a candidate patch and $T = \\{t_1, \\ldots, t_n\\}$ its test suite, where each $t_i$ has label $y_i \\in \\{\\text{\\textsc{Pass}}, \\text{\\textsc{Fail}}\\}$ from execution. Tests decompose into \\emph{fail-to-pass} ($T_{\\text{f2p}}$) and \\emph{pass-to-pass} ($T_{\\text{p2p}}$) subsets; a patch is correct iff $\\forall\\, t_i{:}\\ y_i = \\text{\\textsc{Pass}}$. We compare three formulations for predicting correctness without execution.\n5\t\n6\t\\subsection{Verification Formulations}\n7\t\\label{sec:formulations}\n8\t\n9\tLet $c_p$ denote the code context (unified diff) of patch $p$.\n10\t\n11\t\\paragraph{\\cls{} (per-test classification).} $f(c_p, t_i) \\rightarrow \\hat{y}_i \\in \\{\\text{\\textsc{Pass}}, \\text{\\textsc{Fail}}\\}$ predicts a single binary token per test (cross-entropy loss). Patch verdict: $\\hat{y}_p = \\bigwedge_{i} [\\hat{y}_i = \\text{\\textsc{Pass}}]$. Output cost: one token per test.\n12\t\n13\t\\paragraph{\\cwm{} (output generation).} $g(c_p, t_i) \\rightarrow \\hat{o}_i \\in \\Sigma^*$ generates the execution output string (language modeling loss). Verdict by exact match: $\\hat{y}_i = \\text{\\textsc{Pass}}$ iff $\\hat{o}_i = o_i^*$.\\footnote{Exact string matching is conservative, consistent with \\citet{meta2025cwm}. Semantic equivalences are not counted.} When the generated output is unparseable (i.e., does not conform to the expected structured format), the prediction defaults to the majority class verdict. Output cost: $\\sum_i |\\hat{o}_i|$ tokens.\n14\t\n15\t\\paragraph{\\traj{} (trajectory-level).} $h(c_p, T) \\rightarrow \\hat{y}_p \\in \\{\\text{\\textsc{Resolved}}, \\text{\\textsc{Unresolved}}\\}$ predicts a single patch verdict from all tests, providing no per-test signal. \\traj{} serves as a granularity control: \\cls{}--\\traj{} differences isolate the value of per-test decomposition independent of the classification formulation itself.\n16\t\n17\t\\subsection{Formulation-Specific Failure Modes}\n18\t\\label{sec:failure-modes}\n19\t\n20\tEach formulation has characteristic failure modes that differ in their sensitivity to code complexity:\n21\t\n22\t\\paragraph{Generation failure modes (\\cwm{}).} The open-ended output space introduces three failure modes: (1)~\\emph{token-budget exhaustion}, where $|o_i^*| > B$ guarantees exact-match failure due to truncation; (2)~\\emph{string-value prediction errors}, where semantically equivalent outputs differing in surface form (floating-point precision, dictionary ordering) produce false negatives; and (3)~\\emph{format compliance collapse}, where the model fails to produce structured predictions entirely at high input complexity, even when the generation budget is sufficient. Under optimistic independence, a token sequence of length $L$ with per-token accuracy $q$ has correct-sequence probability $q^L$, making long outputs exponentially harder to predict exactly. We adopt exact string matching following the original \\cwm{} evaluation protocol~\\citep{meta2025cwm}. Unparseable predictions default to the majority-class label (\\textsc{Pass}).\n23\t\n24\t\\paragraph{Classification failure modes (\\cls{}).} The single-token output space eliminates format and truncation failures but constrains the model to encode its reasoning entirely within internal representations, without the intermediate reasoning trace that generation provides. \\cls{} errors concentrate in false negatives (predicting \\textsc{Pass} for failing tests), consistent with a bias toward the majority class.\n25\t\n26\t\\paragraph{Trajectory-level failure modes (\\traj{}).} By collapsing all per-test information into a single binary verdict, \\traj{} discards diagnostic granularity. When a patch fails multiple tests, the trajectory-level formulation cannot identify \\emph{which} tests fail. This limits downstream utility (e.g., targeted re-repair) but avoids per-test aggregation errors.\n27\t\n28\t\\paragraph{Complexity dependence.} These failure modes suggest a complexity-dependent interaction: when execution outputs are short and structured (function-level code), generation modes (1)--(2) are rarely triggered, and all formulations should perform comparably. As output length and structural complexity increase (repository-level code), generation-specific failures may compound, creating divergence. Whether this divergence reflects a fundamental property of the generation formulation or is contingent on model/tokenizer properties is an empirical question we investigate in \\S\\ref{sec:experiments}.\n29\t\n30\t\\subsection{Oracle Accuracy-Utility Framework}\n31\t\\label{sec:oracle}\n32\t\n33\tPer-test granularity is valuable only when per-test predictions are sufficiently accurate and multiple tests provide partially independent signals. Consider a buggy patch with $k = |T_{\\text{f2p}}|$ fail-to-pass tests. A per-test verifier with recall $r$ detects the bug if it identifies at least one failing test:\n34\t%\n35\t\\begin{equation}\n36\t  P_{\\text{detect}}(k) = 1 - (1 - r)^{k_{\\text{eff}}}\n37\t  \\label{eq:pdetect}\n38\t\\end{equation}\n39\t%\n40\twhere $k_{\\text{eff}}$ is the effective number of independent tests. Let $a_h$ denote the trajectory-level detection accuracy; it is independent of $k$.\n41\t\n42\t\\paragraph{Block-correlation model.} Per-test predictions within the same patch are correlated. We model this via the intraclass correlation coefficient $\\rho$ \\citep[ICC;][]{shrout1979icc}:\n43\t%\n44\t\\begin{equation}\n45\t  k_{\\text{eff}} = \\frac{k}{1 + (k - 1)\\rho}\n46\t  \\label{eq:keff}\n47\t\\end{equation}\n48\t%\n49\tWhen $\\rho = 0$, $k_{\\text{eff}} = k$ (full independence); when $\\rho = 1$, $k_{\\text{eff}} = 1$ (per-test collapses to trajectory-level). The per-test advantage relative to a trajectory-level baseline is $\\Delta(k) = P_{\\text{detect}}(k) - a_h$, and the expected population advantage is:\n50\t%\n51\t\\begin{equation}\n52\t  \\mathbb{E}[\\Delta] = \\sum_{k=1}^{K} P(|T_{\\text{f2p}}| = k) \\cdot \\Delta(k)\n53\t  \\label{eq:expected-advantage}\n54\t\\end{equation}\n55\t\n56\t\\paragraph{Testable predictions.} This yields three predictions validated in \\S\\ref{sec:experiments}: (1)~at $k = 1$, $\\Delta \\approx 0$; (2)~$\\Delta(k)$ increases with $k$, dampened by $\\rho > 0$; (3)~$\\mathbb{E}[\\Delta]$ concentrates in multi-F2P-test instances.\n57\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HBi9wSE9U4rowAkjMe1Lag","session_id":"3a86c915-6362-45cf-8a2e-022ec0eb5caa","uuid":"c14e0061-551a-46a7-99aa-0aed3938b09f","timestamp":"2026-05-17T04:17:04.057Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014Li1BqSzZFR8iAoRF55zec","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Qn8L6dBZbc6hyHcM5R3Tah","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2 Round 2 cross-share","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (Novelty + Related Work) in Round 2 of a paper review cross-share. You already completed your Round 1 review and scored 3/6 (Borderline Reject).\n\n## Your Round 1 findings:\n1. [Major, idea-level] Insufficient novelty for top venue\n2. [Major, idea-level] Repo-level results heavily confounded\n3. [Major, presentation-level] LaTeX source vs PDF inconsistencies\nStrengths: controlled design, statistical rigor, honest null results, error complementarity\n\n## Reviewer 1's Round 1 findings (Methods + Experiments):\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Borderline Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\nMajor Issues:\n1. [Major, idea-level] Repo-level results underpowered: DeepSeek CLS does not significantly beat majority baseline (p=0.71). CLS-CWM gap CI [-1.8, 10.2] includes zero. Power is only 0.31 for a 5pp effect with 2 seeds and 48 effective instances. Yet repo-level divergence is framed as a core contribution.\n2. [Major, idea-level] Incomplete three-way comparison at repo-level: Qwen3 repo-level (Table 3) reports only CLS and CWM — TRAJ is entirely absent for the better-performing model. This breaks the paper's \"three formulation comparison\" premise.\n3. [Major, idea-level] \"Tokenizer-specificity\" claim overfit to N=2 models: Qwen3 vs DeepSeek differ in architecture, pretraining, distillation, and tokenizer. Attributing unparseable rate difference to tokenizer requires isolation not provided.\n\nMinor Issues: missing Qwen3 repo-level multi-seed, sparse training details, no error analysis beyond aggregates.\nStrengths: clean controlled design at function-level, appropriate statistics, honest negatives, useful dataset.\n\n## Your Task (Round 2 Supplementary)\n\nFrom your novelty + related work perspective, comment on R1's findings:\n1. R1 flags missing TRAJ on Qwen3 at repo-level. How much does this incomplete comparison weaken the \"systematic comparison\" positioning?\n2. R1 notes tokenizer-specificity claim is overfit to N=2. Is this finding still a useful observation for the field even if causally unsubstantiated?\n3. R1 highlights strong statistical methodology. Does methodological rigor partially compensate for weak novelty?\n\nKeep response to ~200-300 words. Structure as bullet points."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2211,"cache_read_input_tokens":55393,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2211},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a86c915-6362-45cf-8a2e-022ec0eb5caa","uuid":"f2eb3883-af1b-4b39-a4de-9b34141a6618"}
