{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HBi9wSE9U4rowAkjMe1Lag","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my Round 2 supplementary response:\n\n---\n\n**Reviewer 1 (Methods + Experiments) -- Round 2 Supplementary**\n\n**1. R2's claim that repo-level confounds leave the central question unanswered: I largely agree, with nuance.**\n\n- R2 is correct that the paper cannot disentangle formulation from model/tokenizer artifacts at repo level. Table 3 confirms this: all repo-level CWM results are Qwen3-only, DeepSeek rows are blank (\"in progress\"), and no TRAJ repo-level results exist at all. The paper's own text acknowledges this repeatedly (Sec 5.5: \"Whether this reflects a fundamental generation bottleneck or model-specific behavior remains open\").\n- The design is salvageable but requires two things the paper currently lacks: (a) completed DeepSeek repo-level runs for both CLS and CWM (the rows are literally empty in Table 3), and (b) at least one additional seed per repo-level condition. Currently the 4B same-size comparison is single-seed, and CWM 8B is also single-seed. With N=1 per cell, any claim about formulation-level divergence is anecdotal rather than statistical.\n- The function-level cross-model validation (DeepSeek gap 0.02pp) is well-executed and does answer the function-level question convincingly. The problem is exclusively at repo level.\n\n**2. LaTeX/PDF inconsistencies: concerning for reproducibility, not necessarily for data integrity.**\n\n- I cannot verify the specific source-vs-PDF discrepancies R2 flags from the .tex alone. However, the source does contain internal inconsistencies: the abstract reports \"19--26pp\" divergence while Table 3 shows 25.59pp (4B) and 19.31pp (8B), which is consistent. The contribution list says \"217x throughput\" matching Sec 5.6(i). I do not find model-name mismatches in the current .tex source. If these inconsistencies existed in an earlier compiled PDF but have been corrected in the source, R2's concern may be about version control hygiene rather than data fabrication.\n- The empty DeepSeek rows in Table 3 are the more substantive concern: presenting a table with blank cells signals incomplete work, which undermines confidence more than formatting discrepancies.\n\n**3. Is the controlled comparison methodology itself a contribution?**\n\n- Partially, but not at top-venue level in its current state. The controlled design (matched data, matched capacity, multiple seeds, appropriate statistics) is genuinely good experimental methodology. The function-level null result with TOST equivalence and two model families is a clean, publishable finding. The error complementarity analysis (Sec 5.6a) and oracle framework (Sec 3.3) add analytical value.\n- However, methodology alone does not rescue inconclusive results at the paper's claimed headline finding. The title says \"Classification Succeeds Where Generation Stalls,\" but the paper honestly admits it cannot determine whether the stalling is formulation-inherent or model-specific. A controlled comparison that fails to reach a conclusion on its primary research question is a weaker contribution than one that does, regardless of methodological quality.\n- The strongest reframing would be: \"at function level, formulation does not matter (clean result); at repo level, we identify that model/tokenizer properties interact with formulation in ways that practitioners must account for (useful negative finding).\" But this requires the DeepSeek repo-level data to exist.\n\n**Score maintained: 3/6 (Borderline Reject).** The incomplete repo-level cross-model data is the single largest fixable gap. If DeepSeek repo-level results were added with 2+ seeds, this could move to 4/6 regardless of whether the results confirm or contradict the Qwen3 pattern."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a773efb2385874d89 (use SendMessage with to: 'a773efb2385874d89' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 37920\ntool_uses: 9\nduration_ms: 47394</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a86c915-6362-45cf-8a2e-022ec0eb5caa","uuid":"437d4c5e-a315-4bb3-a5d2-ccd0673e801e","timestamp":"2026-05-17T04:17:46.393Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (Methods + Experiments) in Round 2 of a paper review cross-share. You already completed your Round 1 review and scored 3/6 (Borderline Reject).\n\n## Your Round 1 findings:\n1. [Major, idea-level] Repo-level results underpowered: DeepSeek CLS p=0.71 vs majority, power=0.31\n2. [Major, idea-level] Incomplete three-way comparison at repo-level: no Qwen3 TRAJ in Table 3\n3. [Major, idea-level] \"Tokenizer-specificity\" claim overfit to N=2 models\nStrengths: clean controlled design, appropriate statistics (TOST, bootstrap, McNemar), honest negative results, useful dataset (87K labels)\n\n## Reviewer 2's Round 1 findings (Novelty + Related Work):\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Borderline Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\nMajor Issues:\n1. [Major, idea-level] Insufficient novelty for top venue: comparing three known formulations with standard LoRA fine-tuning. Function-level null result is unsurprising. Repo-level divergence (potentially strongest contribution) undermined by confounds.\n2. [Major, idea-level] Repo-level results heavily confounded: paper cannot disentangle formulation from model/tokenizer/training artifacts. CWM collapse attributed to tokenizer on Qwen3, but both CLS and CWM fail on DeepSeek due to training failure. The central question goes unanswered.\n3. [Major, presentation-level] LaTeX source vs PDF inconsistencies: different model names, different experimental completeness, different contribution counts, different efficiency numbers between source files and compiled PDF.\n\nMinor Issues:\n- All citations are (?) placeholders\n- Related Work is thin (~6 references, 3 short paragraphs)\n- Re-repair section promises downstream utility but delivers null\n- Story arc breaks: abstract promises \"actionable guidance\" but repo-level results are inconclusive\n\nStrengths: controlled design, statistical rigor, honest null results, error complementarity finding\n\n## Your Task (Round 2 Supplementary)\n\nFrom your methods + experiments perspective, comment on R2's findings:\n1. R2 says repo-level confounds mean the paper fails to answer its central question. From experimental design perspective, do you agree? Could the design be salvaged?\n2. R2 flags LaTeX/PDF inconsistencies. Does this affect confidence in experimental results?\n3. R2 questions novelty. Is the controlled comparison methodology itself a contribution even with inconclusive results?\n\nKeep response to ~200-300 words. Structure as bullet points.","agentId":"a773efb2385874d89","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my Round 2 supplementary response:\n\n---\n\n**Reviewer 1 (Methods + Experiments) -- Round 2 Supplementary**\n\n**1. R2's claim that repo-level confounds leave the central question unanswered: I largely agree, with nuance.**\n\n- R2 is correct that the paper cannot disentangle formulation from model/tokenizer artifacts at repo level. Table 3 confirms this: all repo-level CWM results are Qwen3-only, DeepSeek rows are blank (\"in progress\"), and no TRAJ repo-level results exist at all. The paper's own text acknowledges this repeatedly (Sec 5.5: \"Whether this reflects a fundamental generation bottleneck or model-specific behavior remains open\").\n- The design is salvageable but requires two things the paper currently lacks: (a) completed DeepSeek repo-level runs for both CLS and CWM (the rows are literally empty in Table 3), and (b) at least one additional seed per repo-level condition. Currently the 4B same-size comparison is single-seed, and CWM 8B is also single-seed. With N=1 per cell, any claim about formulation-level divergence is anecdotal rather than statistical.\n- The function-level cross-model validation (DeepSeek gap 0.02pp) is well-executed and does answer the function-level question convincingly. The problem is exclusively at repo level.\n\n**2. LaTeX/PDF inconsistencies: concerning for reproducibility, not necessarily for data integrity.**\n\n- I cannot verify the specific source-vs-PDF discrepancies R2 flags from the .tex alone. However, the source does contain internal inconsistencies: the abstract reports \"19--26pp\" divergence while Table 3 shows 25.59pp (4B) and 19.31pp (8B), which is consistent. The contribution list says \"217x throughput\" matching Sec 5.6(i). I do not find model-name mismatches in the current .tex source. If these inconsistencies existed in an earlier compiled PDF but have been corrected in the source, R2's concern may be about version control hygiene rather than data fabrication.\n- The empty DeepSeek rows in Table 3 are the more substantive concern: presenting a table with blank cells signals incomplete work, which undermines confidence more than formatting discrepancies.\n\n**3. Is the controlled comparison methodology itself a contribution?**\n\n- Partially, but not at top-venue level in its current state. The controlled design (matched data, matched capacity, multiple seeds, appropriate statistics) is genuinely good experimental methodology. The function-level null result with TOST equivalence and two model families is a clean, publishable finding. The error complementarity analysis (Sec 5.6a) and oracle framework (Sec 3.3) add analytical value.\n- However, methodology alone does not rescue inconclusive results at the paper's claimed headline finding. The title says \"Classification Succeeds Where Generation Stalls,\" but the paper honestly admits it cannot determine whether the stalling is formulation-inherent or model-specific. A controlled comparison that fails to reach a conclusion on its primary research question is a weaker contribution than one that does, regardless of methodological quality.\n- The strongest reframing would be: \"at function level, formulation does not matter (clean result); at repo level, we identify that model/tokenizer properties interact with formulation in ways that practitioners must account for (useful negative finding).\" But this requires the DeepSeek repo-level data to exist.\n\n**Score maintained: 3/6 (Borderline Reject).** The incomplete repo-level cross-model data is the single largest fixable gap. If DeepSeek repo-level results were added with 2+ seeds, this could move to 4/6 regardless of whether the results confirm or contradict the Qwen3 pattern."}],"totalDurationMs":47394,"totalTokens":37920,"totalToolUseCount":9,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":691,"cache_read_input_tokens":36332,"output_tokens":894,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":691},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":894,"cache_read_input_tokens":36332,"cache_creation_input_tokens":691,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":691,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":8,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":1}}}
