{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qn8L6dBZbc6hyHcM5R3Tah","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my Round 2 supplementary response:\n\n---\n\n**Reviewer 2 (Novelty + Related Work) -- Round 2 Supplementary**\n\n**Re: R1's Q1 -- Missing TRAJ on Qwen3 at repo-level and the \"systematic comparison\" positioning**\n\n- This is the most damaging gap from a novelty standpoint. The paper's core positioning is \"first systematic comparison of three formulations.\" Dropping TRAJ entirely at repo-level reduces the headline contribution to a two-way CLS-vs-CWM ablation at the complexity level where results are most interesting and most relevant to practitioners. The Related Work explicitly positions against SWE-RM (trajectory-level); without repo-level TRAJ data, the paper cannot substantiate its differentiation from that line of work. A two-way comparison at repo-level is an incremental ablation study, not a systematic three-way comparison. This alone undermines the paper's primary novelty claim.\n\n**Re: R1's Q2 -- Tokenizer-specificity observation at N=2**\n\n- As a descriptive observation it has modest value: practitioners benefit from knowing generation-based verification may behave differently across model families. But architecture, pretraining corpus, distillation strategy, and tokenizer are fully confounded at N=2. The paper cites no tokenizer analysis literature that would ground the attribution. As written, this is a hypothesis presented with the rhetorical weight of a finding. Downgrading to an explicit \"hypothesis for future work\" would be honest and still useful. Currently it inflates the contribution list without evidential support.\n\n**Re: R1's Q3 -- Does methodological rigor compensate for weak novelty?**\n\n- Rigor is necessary but insufficient. The statistical machinery (clustered bootstrap, TOST, ICC, honest null results) is commendable and genuinely rare in this subfield. Reviewers can trust the reported numbers. However, strong methodology applied to an incomplete experimental matrix yields a well-executed pilot study, not a venue-ready empirical contribution. I would consider upgrading from 3 to 4 if missing conditions (repo-level TRAJ, DeepSeek repo-level) were filled and claims scoped to match actual evidence. Rigor alone does not close the coverage gap.\n\n**Score: 3/6 (unchanged).** Methodological quality is real; incomplete experimental matrix and overclaimed tokenizer finding prevent an upgrade."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a64c9d12360c9ea71 (use SendMessage with to: 'a64c9d12360c9ea71' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 31322\ntool_uses: 6\nduration_ms: 44299</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a86c915-6362-45cf-8a2e-022ec0eb5caa","uuid":"45b648e9-118c-4d35-9fdd-429b89879828","timestamp":"2026-05-17T04:17:48.986Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (Novelty + Related Work) in Round 2 of a paper review cross-share. You already completed your Round 1 review and scored 3/6 (Borderline Reject).\n\n## Your Round 1 findings:\n1. [Major, idea-level] Insufficient novelty for top venue\n2. [Major, idea-level] Repo-level results heavily confounded\n3. [Major, presentation-level] LaTeX source vs PDF inconsistencies\nStrengths: controlled design, statistical rigor, honest null results, error complementarity\n\n## Reviewer 1's Round 1 findings (Methods + Experiments):\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Borderline Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\nMajor Issues:\n1. [Major, idea-level] Repo-level results underpowered: DeepSeek CLS does not significantly beat majority baseline (p=0.71). CLS-CWM gap CI [-1.8, 10.2] includes zero. Power is only 0.31 for a 5pp effect with 2 seeds and 48 effective instances. Yet repo-level divergence is framed as a core contribution.\n2. [Major, idea-level] Incomplete three-way comparison at repo-level: Qwen3 repo-level (Table 3) reports only CLS and CWM — TRAJ is entirely absent for the better-performing model. This breaks the paper's \"three formulation comparison\" premise.\n3. [Major, idea-level] \"Tokenizer-specificity\" claim overfit to N=2 models: Qwen3 vs DeepSeek differ in architecture, pretraining, distillation, and tokenizer. Attributing unparseable rate difference to tokenizer requires isolation not provided.\n\nMinor Issues: missing Qwen3 repo-level multi-seed, sparse training details, no error analysis beyond aggregates.\nStrengths: clean controlled design at function-level, appropriate statistics, honest negatives, useful dataset.\n\n## Your Task (Round 2 Supplementary)\n\nFrom your novelty + related work perspective, comment on R1's findings:\n1. R1 flags missing TRAJ on Qwen3 at repo-level. How much does this incomplete comparison weaken the \"systematic comparison\" positioning?\n2. R1 notes tokenizer-specificity claim is overfit to N=2. Is this finding still a useful observation for the field even if causally unsubstantiated?\n3. R1 highlights strong statistical methodology. Does methodological rigor partially compensate for weak novelty?\n\nKeep response to ~200-300 words. Structure as bullet points.","agentId":"a64c9d12360c9ea71","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my Round 2 supplementary response:\n\n---\n\n**Reviewer 2 (Novelty + Related Work) -- Round 2 Supplementary**\n\n**Re: R1's Q1 -- Missing TRAJ on Qwen3 at repo-level and the \"systematic comparison\" positioning**\n\n- This is the most damaging gap from a novelty standpoint. The paper's core positioning is \"first systematic comparison of three formulations.\" Dropping TRAJ entirely at repo-level reduces the headline contribution to a two-way CLS-vs-CWM ablation at the complexity level where results are most interesting and most relevant to practitioners. The Related Work explicitly positions against SWE-RM (trajectory-level); without repo-level TRAJ data, the paper cannot substantiate its differentiation from that line of work. A two-way comparison at repo-level is an incremental ablation study, not a systematic three-way comparison. This alone undermines the paper's primary novelty claim.\n\n**Re: R1's Q2 -- Tokenizer-specificity observation at N=2**\n\n- As a descriptive observation it has modest value: practitioners benefit from knowing generation-based verification may behave differently across model families. But architecture, pretraining corpus, distillation strategy, and tokenizer are fully confounded at N=2. The paper cites no tokenizer analysis literature that would ground the attribution. As written, this is a hypothesis presented with the rhetorical weight of a finding. Downgrading to an explicit \"hypothesis for future work\" would be honest and still useful. Currently it inflates the contribution list without evidential support.\n\n**Re: R1's Q3 -- Does methodological rigor compensate for weak novelty?**\n\n- Rigor is necessary but insufficient. The statistical machinery (clustered bootstrap, TOST, ICC, honest null results) is commendable and genuinely rare in this subfield. Reviewers can trust the reported numbers. However, strong methodology applied to an incomplete experimental matrix yields a well-executed pilot study, not a venue-ready empirical contribution. I would consider upgrading from 3 to 4 if missing conditions (repo-level TRAJ, DeepSeek repo-level) were filled and claims scoped to match actual evidence. Rigor alone does not close the coverage gap.\n\n**Score: 3/6 (unchanged).** Methodological quality is real; incomplete experimental matrix and overclaimed tokenizer finding prevent an upgrade."}],"totalDurationMs":44299,"totalTokens":31322,"totalToolUseCount":6,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1267,"cache_read_input_tokens":29497,"output_tokens":555,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1267},"inferen